  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004908",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All 10 candidates are direct matches to the target trait (kidney cancer) via trait_reported/trait_efo/phenotyping_reported (including renal carcinoma/renal cell carcinoma as aligned concepts). Among them, PGS004908 has the strongest visible PRS-relevant performance evidence: it reports the highest full-model AUROC (0.74, EUR evaluation) and a strong per-SD effect size (OR=1.5, 95% CI 1.43–1.58), which is materially stronger than other candidates’ per-SD effects (generally ~1.15–1.24 or unspecified). Endpoint fidelity is good (phenotyping_reported: Kidney cancer) and covariates are standard epidemiologic/demographic (age, sex, PCs, BMI, smoking, hypertension), avoiding obvious high-leakage clinical packaging. Main limitation: none of the candidates report clearly PRS-only AUROC/R2, so comparisons rely on effect size and full-model AUROC (which can be covariate-influenced), lowering certainty despite PGS004908’s clear edge on the available metrics."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_SUB_OPTIMAL",
  "best_model_id": "PGS002738",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct/near-direct matches for the target (alcohol dependence) via alcohol use disorder / alcohol dependence measurement. PGS002738 is the best-supported because its trait fields are most directly aligned (trait_reported: Alcohol use disorder; trait_efo: alcohol dependence; phenotyping_reported: Alcohol use disorder) and it provides the strongest discriminative evidence among candidates (OR top 10% vs rest = 1.96, 95% CI 1.54–2.51) in a sizeable EUR evaluation (n=7,900) with development cohorts including MVP and UKB. The main limitation is that covariates are listed as Unknown and the metric is not a PRS-only AUC/R2, while the alternative PGS000201/PGS000202 report only small ΔR2 values (0.01192 and 0.00456) and are framed as 'problematic alcohol use' despite DSM-5 AUD criteria phenotyping; thus, evidence is sufficient to prefer PGS002738 but not strong enough for a high-confidence designation."
}

{
  "outcome": "DIRECT_HIGH_QUALITY",
  "best_model_id": "PGS004911",
  "confidence": "Moderate",
  "rationale": "All four candidates are direct matches to hypertrophic cardiomyopathy by trait_efo, but PGS000778 has weaker endpoint fidelity because phenotyping_reported is restricted to “clinical events in individuals with a pathogenic or likely pathogenic sarcomeric variant” and has small validation (n=368). PGS000739 reports only a full-model AUROC (0.821) while using substantial clinical covariates (obesity, HTN, AF, CAD, rare-variant carrier status), so its discrimination is likely covariate-inflated and not PRS-comparable per the provided domain guidance. The 2025 PRS-CS models (PGS004910 and PGS004911) have clean disease phenotyping_reported (“Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy”) with basic covariates (age, age^2, sex, PCs) and identical validation size (n=343,182) in EUR. Between them, PGS004911 (MTAG) is mildly preferred because MTAG is explicitly endorsed in the domain knowledge as a power-boosting enhancement for the same disease endpoint, and it shows stronger reported performance metrics than PGS004910 (full-model AUROC 0.80 vs 0.73; R² 0.048 vs 0.031). Confidence is Moderate because the reported AUROCs are full-model (not explicitly PGS-only), and no incremental/PRS-only AUROC is provided to isolate the PRS contribution."
}

